From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:59:40 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] util: let the embedding host supply the allocator

Mesa's shader compiles allocate millions of small blocks from NIR's
ralloc, the util containers and the compiler passes, and free them again
when a context or a screen goes. On Darwin they all come from the
process's default malloc zone, which holds on to what a boot's peak
needed and reports it back to the kernel on its own schedule. A host
that restarts a guest in its own process cannot make the process
footprint return to where it was, however carefully it keeps its own
state out of that zone: routing only ralloc's callers to another heap
still left the zone's reclaimable pool flipping from one restart to the
next, and routing every C allocation Mesa makes did not.

Add -Dhost-heap, off by default and Darwin only. It builds
u_host_heap.c, which defines malloc, calloc, realloc, posix_memalign,
aligned_alloc and free with hidden visibility, and each shared library
that takes its allocator from the host (libgallium, libEGL and
libvulkan_kosmickrisp) links it as a whole. The static linker then binds
every reference to those names inside the image to the definitions:
calls, a free passed as a destructor, and the inline util_dynarray code
wherever it lands, in C, C++ and Objective-C alike. Every other image
keeps libSystem's, and so do C++ new and the blocks libSystem makes for
Mesa (strdup, asprintf, realpath). No call site changes, so nothing is
left to rebase with a new Mesa.

Until a host calls the exported mesa_set_host_allocator the definitions
forward to libmalloc's zone API, so a build that never installs one
behaves as it did. The installation is once per image and the callbacks
take the pointers they did not allocate to libSystem, which is how a
block made before installation, or by libSystem, is still freed
correctly. The test executable links the allocator the same way and
checks the forwarding, the entry points' edge cases, a free taken as a
function pointer, a foreign block, failure as ENOMEM and a block freed
on another thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
